    "landing_faq_6W7fCC": {
      "type": "landing-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "question_BAc9wV": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What ages is the kit for?",
            "answer": "<p>Roughly ages 1–12. Not every mission is right for every age, so the book includes guidance to help you choose. With toddlers, you take the lead. Older kids can help plan, make decisions, and occasionally inform you that your entire approach is wrong.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_3sxudz": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What’s included in the kit?",
            "answer": "<p>The complete kit includes:</p><ul><li>A physical <strong>softcover</strong> book with <strong>28 tear-out, unfoldable missions</strong> and step-by-step instructions.</li><li><strong>28 premium gold-foiled trophy stickers</strong> to award for completed missions.</li><li>The <strong>Dad Achievement Poster</strong> to collect those trophies and display your shared wins.</li><li><strong>A letter from Santa</strong>, tucked into its special envelope.</li></ul><p>It’s a physical kit, not a download or a book of generic parenting tips. Dad glory comes in the package. Actual glory requires a little work.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_ZSapzE": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do the missions, stickers, and poster work together?",
            "answer": "<p>Pick a mission. Tear out and unfold its page. Do the activity together. When you’ve earned it, your kid awards you a premium gold-foiled trophy sticker, which goes on the Dad Achievement Poster.</p><p>Hang the poster where your kid can see it. Every sticker marks something you did together. Every empty space is another mission waiting to happen. Your kid might start requesting the next mission without you having to pitch it.</p><p>A reward system for you. Finally, someone understands management.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_EsXUPJ": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why are the pages meant to be torn out?",
            "answer": "<p>Because they’re mission sheets, not sacred manuscripts. Tear one out, unfold it, and bring the instructions wherever the action takes you. Keep it afterward if you want another round.</p><p>It’s one of the few books where missing pages mean you’re doing it right.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_Mr8P0k": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can we do the missions more than once?",
            "answer": "<p>Of course. The page gets torn out once. The mission can happen as many times as you like. Keep the instructions and repeat a favorite, or change it up as your kid grows.</p><p>If your pirate career is going well, there’s no need to retire after one voyage.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_efwaut": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is this going to make a huge mess?",
            "answer": "<p>Depends on the mission. Some leave almost no evidence. Others may require a towel, a protected table, and a convincing explanation for the other parent.</p><p>Check the supplies and setup first. You and your kid can tackle the cleanup together, too. Apparently that’s also quality time. Terrible news.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_T7LY0N": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if I’m not crafty or I feel ridiculous doing this stuff?",
            "answer": "<p>Good. You’re qualified.</p><p>The missions come with instructions, so you don’t need to be an artist, an engineer, or a suspiciously perfect Instagram dad. Start with something comfortable and go from there.</p><p>If the paper boat is crooked, see if it floats. Your kid isn’t judging the craftsmanship. Well, not <em>officially</em>.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_KNJNjy": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do we need special tools or a trip to Home Depot?",
            "answer": "<p>Most missions use things you probably have around the house. Some need extra supplies or a bit of preparation, so each mission tells you what to check before starting.</p><p>Start with the kitchen drawers and the closet. If you still want a trip to Home Depot, you’ll need a different excuse.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_cIFk71": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Are the missions safe?",
            "answer": "<p>You choose activities appropriate for your child’s age and abilities, read the instructions, and supervise throughout. Anything involving water, cooking, tools, or small objects needs the right precautions.</p><p>If a mission doesn’t suit your kid or your space, adapt it safely or pick another. The Dad of the Year trophy is not worth a trip to the ER.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_xHETMO": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if my kid gets bored halfway through?",
            "answer": "<p>Start with a shorter mission. Let your kid make a decision or two. If an activity isn’t clicking, change it, take a break, or try a different one later.</p><p>You don’t have to complete all 28 in order. Even pirates take days off.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_0fj2hS": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How long do the missions take? What if I’m busy?",
            "answer": "<p>Some are quick activities, while others take more time or preparation. The mission guides help you check the time, supplies, and setup before choosing one that fits your day.</p><p>You don’t need to do them in order, and nobody expects all 28 by Sunday night. This is fatherhood, not a quarterly performance review.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_mbsKN6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why buy it now instead of someday?",
            "answer": "<p>Because “when things calm down” has a funny way of turning into next month, next year, and “Wait, when did you get so big?”</p><p>This isn’t about rushing through 28 missions. It’s about having a plan ready when you and your kid get an afternoon together. Start with one. Your rise to glory can be gradual.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_2CqaAV": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is this another parenting advice book?",
            "answer": "<p>Nope. It’s a book you <em>use</em>, not one that tells you everything you’re doing wrong. Inside are 28 guided missions. Pick one, tear out and unfold the page, follow the steps, and get into it with your kid.</p><p>We skipped the parenting lectures. The internet has that department fully staffed.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_vuT7yr": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What do my kid and I actually get out of this?",
            "answer": "<p>Your kid gets your attention, a say in the mission, and someone willing to join the nonsense. You get 28 ready-made plans instead of having to invent an entire childhood every Saturday morning.</p><p>Together you collect inside jokes, small victories, and stories that belong to you both. You also get physical evidence that you occasionally know what you’re doing.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_QTrzRN": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why is this better than buying another toy?",
            "answer": "<p>Because you come with it.</p><p>A toy gives your kid something to play with. This kit gives you and your kid 28 things to do <em>together</em>, with instructions to get the fun started and a poster to celebrate what you did.</p><p>Your kid probably owns enough toys. A chance to review your performance as a pirate? That’s new.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_HDBQkL": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Will my kid actually remember this?",
            "answer": "<p>We can’t promise which moments will stick. But doing something ridiculous together gives you a story, and the sticker-covered poster gives you a reason to tell it again. Remember the disastrous pancake masterpiece? The highly suspicious pirate treasure?</p><p>That’s the idea: more “Remember when we…” and fewer Saturdays nobody can remember.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_gaWZTD": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Will my kid even care about the poster?",
            "answer": "<p>The poster is a record of missions your kid helped complete, with shiny trophy stickers they get to award. Let them add the sticker, tell you their favorite part, and choose what to try next.</p><p>Hang it somewhere they’ll see it. There’s no guarantee every kid becomes a sticker fanatic, but seeing your shared wins can be a pretty good reason to ask, “Can we do another one?”</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_ybnf1o": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why are the stickers important? Can I get extras?",
            "answer": "<p>The gold-foiled stickers are trophies, not filler. After a mission, your kid gets to award one and add it to the poster. That’s how an afternoon of silliness becomes a little ceremony, with a visible reminder of what you did together.</p><p>Want another child to have their own trophy wall? Check out the <a href=\"https://bestdadbook.com/products/epic-dad-achievement-poster-sticker-pack-bundle\">extra poster and sticker bundle</a> for availability.</p><p>One dad. Multiple performance evaluations.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_sBAjm4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What does my kid get out of it?",
            "answer": "<p>A dad who’s ready to get involved. Your kid gets to explore, make decisions, celebrate completed missions, and see their wins on the poster.</p><p>The writing is for you. The experience belongs to both of you. And your kid gets to hand out the trophies, which is a frankly alarming amount of authority.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_pBfY86": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can moms, grandmas, and grandpas do the missions too?",
            "answer": "<p>Absolutely. The book is written for dads, but moms, grandparents, and other grown-ups can join in. We’re not checking birth certificates before anyone builds a living-room fort.</p><p>Dad gets the title. Everyone can get in on the fun.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_AUuw9j": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Where is the book printed, how long is shipping, and what if I need to return it?",
            "answer": "<p>The book is <strong>printed in the EU</strong>. We ship internationally, including to the <strong>United States and Canada</strong>. Delivery times and shipping costs depend on the destination and are shown through our ordering process. See the <a href=\"https://bestdadbook.com/policies/shipping-policy\">Shipping Policy</a> for details. If you need the kit by a specific date, contact <a href=\"mailto:info@bestdadbook.com\">info@bestdadbook.com</a> before placing your order.</p><p>Changed your mind? Eligible unused, unopened kits can be returned under our <strong>30-day voluntary return policy</strong>, including sale purchases. For US and Canadian orders, returns are sent back to Slovenia at the customer’s expense. EU customers also have separate statutory withdrawal rights. See the full <a href=\"https://bestdadbook.com/policies/refund-policy\">Return Policy</a> for conditions, deadlines, and exceptions.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
